window.docContents[118].resolve({"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L5-___-The-oscillation-dies-out--AI-assistance-contract":{"contents":"* Keep the lesson independent and use nominal Osc | A | B identity.* Treat records under one name as states, configurations, or rival estimates of one\nsystem; compare them, and refuse different names.* Keep Body, Spring, and Damper as designated particulars. Measure γ on the\ndamper and m on the body, while retaining the whole oscillator as the bearer of both;\nplace δ and the regime on the whole.* Apply PhysLib laws to r.osc; every accepted HCP quantity must compute from the\nlesson's own state fields.* Keep accepted formulas at the IndividualQuantity level; do not drop the oscillator\nbearer to build the decay-rate quotient.* Keep the γ kind distinct from ISO 3-24 damping coefficient, even though the\nupstream formula relates them.* Compare δ and ω₀ by explicit magnitudes when showing the underdamped criterion;\ndo not present their kinded comparison as licensed.* Do not change ForPhysLib, PKC core, PhysLib, or Tutorial.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L5 — The oscillation dies out","header":"5.6.8. AI assistance contract","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L5-___-The-oscillation-dies-out--AI-assistance-contract"}});